---
import "../styles/about.css";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href, formatDate, loadManifest, loadSite, safeUrl } from "../lib/data.js";

const sources = loadManifest();
const site = loadSite();
const sourceById = Object.fromEntries(sources.map((s) => [s.id, s]));
---
<Base title="About & editions" section="about" description="How Res Publica's texts are imported, how passages are referenced, and how connections are reviewed.">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>How this library works</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Res Publica is a free, nonpartisan, open-source library. It is built so that you can check every claim it
        makes: where each text came from, how each passage is numbered, and who connected two passages and why.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="container about">
    <nav class="about-nav" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#texts">Texts</a><a href="#refs">References</a><a href="#connections">Connections</a><a href="#editions">Editions</a><a href="#ocr">Scans</a><a href="#privacy">Privacy</a><a href="#open">Open source</a>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="texts">Where the texts come from</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Download.</strong> Each source is downloaded once from a public-domain original, and its address, date and checksum are recorded.</li>
        <li><strong>Import.</strong> A program splits the original into passages. It may collapse spacing and remove markup, but never changes a word, a spelling or a mark of punctuation. Original spellings such as “chuse” and “defence” stay.</li>
        <li><strong>Verify.</strong> Before anything is published, every import is run again and must reproduce the published text exactly, so no passage can be retyped or “corrected” by hand.</li>
        <li><strong>Flag, don't fix.</strong> Suspected transcription errors are listed in a public errata log for a person to check against a page image.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="refs">Every passage has a permanent address</h2>
      <p>References are short names that never change once published. Link to one, cite one, or type one into search.</p>
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Reference</th><th scope="col">Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a class="ref-chip" href={href("/declaration/#Declaration.2")}>Declaration.2</a></td><td>The Declaration of Independence, second paragraph (“We hold these truths…”)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a class="ref-chip" href={href("/constitution/#Constitution.Preamble")}>Constitution.Preamble</a></td><td>The Preamble</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a class="ref-chip" href={href("/constitution/#Constitution.1.8.3")}>Constitution.1.8.3</a></td><td>Article I, Section 8, Clause 3</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a class="ref-chip" href={href("/constitution/#Constitution.6.2")}>Constitution.6.2</a></td><td>Article VI, Clause 2 (Articles V–VII have no sections)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a class="ref-chip" href={href("/amendments/#Amendment.14.1")}>Amendment.14.1</a></td><td>Amendment XIV, Section 1</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a class="ref-chip" href={href("/federalist/10/#Federalist.10.1")}>Federalist.10.1</a></td><td>The Federalist No. 10, first paragraph</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a class="ref-chip" href={href("/cato/3/#Cato.3.2")}>Cato.3.2</a></td><td>The third letter of Cato, second paragraph</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a class="ref-chip" href={href("/caution/#Caution.1")}>Caution.1</a></td><td>A single essay (“Caution”), first paragraph</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="connections">Connections and review</h2>
      <p>
        A connection says how two passages relate: one <em>quotes</em>, <em>cites</em>, <em>responds to</em> or
        <em>rebuts</em> another, a case <em>interprets</em> a clause, and so on. Each one records why it was made,
        who or what made it, and whether a person has reviewed it.
      </p>
      <ul class="legend">
        <li><span class="badge suggested">Suggested</span> Proposed by a machine and not yet reviewed by a person. Shown with a dashed border.</li>
        <li><span class="badge reviewed">Reviewed</span> Checked by a person.</li>
        <li><span class="badge overruled">Overruled</span> A case that a later decision overruled, recorded with a source for that fact.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Any quotation shown in a connection is checked by code against the text, letter for letter.</p>

      <h2 id="editions">Editions</h2>
      {site.works.filter((w) => w.collection !== "ratification").map((w) => (
        <section class="edition card">
          <h3>{w.title}</h3>
          <p class="muted">{w.edition.name}{w.date && ` · ${formatDate(w.date)}`}</p>
          <p>{w.edition.notes}</p>
          <ul class="src-list">
            {w.source_ids.map((id) => {
              const s = sourceById[id];
              const url = s && safeUrl(s.url);
              return s && (
                <li>
                  {url ? <a href={url} rel="noopener noreferrer">{s.notes || s.id}</a> : (s.notes || s.id)}
                  <span class="muted"> Retrieved {formatDate(s.retrieved_at)}. {s.license}</span>
                </li>
              );
            })}
          </ul>
        </section>
      ))}

      <h2 id="ocr">The ratification debate: texts read from scans</h2>
      <p>
        The essays and letters of the ratification debate come from three collections printed in 1888 and 1892, now
        in the public domain. No accurate digital text of them existed, so Res Publica reads them from the Internet
        Archive's scans of the printed books:
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Layout.</strong> One scan's OCR gives the words and their place on the page. Running heads, page numbers and footnotes are set apart, and a paragraph broken by a page is joined again.</li>
        <li><strong>A second reading.</strong> Each word is compared with the OCR of another scan of the same printed edition, made from a different copy of the book.</li>
        <li><strong>A vote, with limits.</strong> Where the scans differ, the reading most scans share wins, or the one that is an English word found in a clean text. A real word is never swapped for a different real word, and figures that differ are never settled by vote.</li>
        <li><strong>Doubt is shown.</strong> Words no rule settles are kept as the main scan reads them and <span class="unc">underlined with dots</span>. Select the paragraph to see each scan's reading and a link to the page image.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        No word is typed by hand. Errors that every scan shares cannot be caught this way, and the newspaper headings,
        set in display type, are read worst; please report what you find. Which side a writer took is Res Publica's
        grouping; authors are as the editions give them, and several of those attributions are disputed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Brutus, one of the most important Anti-Federalist writers, is not here yet: none of these collections prints his
        essays, and no other public-domain printing has been found that can be imported and checked.
      </p>
      {[...new Set(site.works.filter((w) => w.collection === "ratification").map((w) => w.edition.name))].map((name) => {
        const ws = site.works.filter((w) => w.collection === "ratification" && w.edition.name === name);
        return (
          <section class="edition card">
            <h3>{name}</h3>
            <p class="muted">{ws.map((w) => w.title).join(" · ")}</p>
            <ul class="src-list">
              {ws[0].source_ids.map((id) => {
                const s = sourceById[id];
                const url = s && safeUrl(s.url);
                return s && (
                  <li>
                    {url ? <a href={url} rel="noopener noreferrer">{s.notes || s.id}</a> : (s.notes || s.id)}
                    <span class="muted"> Retrieved {formatDate(s.retrieved_at)}. {s.license}</span>
                  </li>
                );
              })}
            </ul>
          </section>
        );
      })}

      <h2 id="cases">Supreme Court cases</h2>
      <p>
        Which cases are here, and which clauses they are linked to, come from the Constitution Annotated, the
        Library of Congress's analysis of the Constitution (2022 print edition), never from anyone's judgment of a
        case's importance. The first set is the case its essays cite most in each topic, every case they cite on ten or
        more pages, and every decision one of those cases overruled according to its Table of Supreme Court Decisions
        Overruled by Subsequent Decisions.
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>The case.</strong> Each case is identified by its CourtListener cluster (Free Law Project), found by its U.S. Reports citation. The full opinion is a link away, on CourtListener.</li>
        <li><strong>The syllabus.</strong> The Reporter of Decisions' syllabus is copied as printed in the United States Reports, from the text layer of the Library of Congress's scan of each case. Older volumes were read by OCR and have not been checked word by word; each paragraph links to its page image. Where the syllabus could not be read reliably, or the volume predates the official syllabus, none is shown and the page says why.</li>
        <li><strong>The links.</strong> A case “interprets” a provision where the Constitution Annotated's essays on that provision cite it on at least two pages; an “overrules” link comes only from the table of overruled decisions. Each link names its source and page, and awaits a person's review like every other link.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Res Publica never says in its own words what a case held, or whether it is still good law.
      </p>

      <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        No accounts, no cookies, no analytics, and no third-party scripts. Your color theme and text size are kept
        only in your own browser.
      </p>

      <h2 id="open">Open source</h2>
      <p>
        The code is MIT-licensed; connections and editorial content are CC BY-SA 4.0; the source texts are in the
        public domain. Everything, including the checks described here, is on
        <a href="https://github.com/jackhale98/respublica" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
